\pard\sb200\sa180\b\fs31 Business Requirements: Catalogue, Order Messaging and Store Discovery\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 Source: \\BR_EXAMPLE.docx\\ from the author's original STORAGE collection (v1.0.0, 2025-09-04). The source juxtaposes three business-requirement examples and a small PIM requirement table. This edition retains the business intent but removes a named retailer and system-specific identifiers.\b0\f0\par
\pard\sb190\sa100\b\fs25 Source guidance: what a business requirement is\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 The original says a business requirement should:\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 1. Correspond to a specific business goal served by the system.\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 2. Avoid prescribing low-level implementation.\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 3. Stay within the agreed project scope.\b0\f0\par
\pard\sb190\sa100\b\fs25 Example A \u8212? Product Information Management\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 The original includes these requirement IDs:\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 ID    |    Business need stated in source\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 PIM21    |    Provide an internal product catalogue\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 PIM22    |    Support finding products by attributes such as season and article number\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 PIM23    |    Support a hierarchy such as product \u8594? model-color \u8594? model, with inherited configuration and level-specific descriptions\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 Review addition: Verify which catalogue hierarchy is owned by the business and how overrides/inheritance should behave before assigning technical schemas.\b0\f0\par
\pard\sb190\sa100\b\fs25 Example B \u8212? Order notifications for fulfillment staff\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 The source proposes a dedicated channel notifying warehouse/order-assembly staff about orders with the \u8220?Auto\u8221? receipt type. The original names a specific retailer's system, omitted here.\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 - Business goal: Ensure assembly staff receive relevant orders without having to search multiple systems.\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 - In scope: Event selection, delivery of order data, visibility of failed delivery.\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 - Acceptance consideration (added): Every eligible order is forwarded no more than once at business-effect level, and rejected notifications are observable.\b0\f0\par
\pard\sb190\sa100\b\fs25 Example C \u8212? Store data and location discovery\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 The source includes two customer-value statements: correctly formalized store information on the website, reducing checkout blockers; and map location centered on the customer's city/town to reduce navigation steps.\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 Objective    |    Suggested measurable acceptance (new)\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 Reliable store data    |    Test required store information fields and checkout mapping\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 City-first map discovery    |    Measure steps from opening location finder to selecting a store\b0\f0\par
\pard\sb190\sa100\b\fs25 Translating to architecture\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 The examples distinguish outcome-focused business requirements from follow-on functional and integration specifications. A real solution might later need a product catalogue service, event-driven fulfillment integration or geolocation/branch API; those are design possibilities, not requirements mandated by the original source.\b0\f0\par
